[Music]  if we were to ever encounter alien life  would we be able to recognize it as life  at all if an organism lived and breathed  like animals on earth we'd certainly  identify it as alive but what if some  aliens don't look like organic life as  we know it or aren't organic in the  first place a project that expands the  horizons of what life on other planets  might look like is the teeming universe  an interstellar field guide by  speculative author and artist christian  klein this book which i have a link to  down below is one i've covered a section  of before on this channel on our  previous expedition through the cosmos  we saw all manner of incredible species  and learned about the science behind  them for this entry into the archive  we'll be journeying on a deep space  vessel called the peregrine to even more  extreme environments  we'll start with planets where the  organisms are truly strange and as we  continue  we'll reach places where we might need a  whole new definition of life  in a far-flung corner of the galaxy our  first stop is a planet shrouded in a  haze of dust this is aite an extreme  world with continent-sized dust storms  that keep the surface in near permanent  twilight  stepping out onto the surface we find a  shadow world plants on ait possess black  leaves to absorb as much of the sun's  energy as possible and animals have  gotten even more creative to adapt the  low light the first life form we  encounter on our voyage is a hella renee  a wolf sized ambush predator that stalks  the dim grasslands in search of prey but  what's most unusual about the heller  rene is that unlike almost all animals  they possess three eyes two on the side  and a massive one in the middle this  might seem far-fetched yet certain  species of real amphibians and reptiles  possess tiny photoreceptive eyes called  solar eyes in the center of their head  these third eyes aren't as complex as  their primary ones but are excellent at  detecting light so it's likely that the  hellerenne's third eye began as solar  eye spots but due to planet aeite's  dimness became their dominant visual  organ  and other animals on aite have followed  this trend the sconics are moose-sized  galloping herbivores that share the  ait's three-eyed setup with two eyes  having moved to the side to keep watch  for predators the sconex graze in large  family herds digging vegetation from the  ground with their wedge-shaped beaks and  ready to dart at the first sign of  trouble in many ways these majestic  animals are the wild horses of these  planes  a somewhat less majestic life form is  the sand pig these round near-sighted  animals spend their time shuffling along  the plains foraging for the worm-like  creatures they prefer to feed on these  burrowing animals spend much of their  time tunneling snugly underground only  surfacing in the low light of dawn to  shuffle over and visit the burrows of  other sand pigs and i have to say i'm a  huge fan of these round creatures as we  climb back aboard our spaceship a trio  of baby helerene's watcher departure  these infants are called pupples and  they're far less menacing than their  carnivorous parents they show us that  although aite is a planet of dust and  dimness the beauty of life can still  take hold  from orbit our next planet solis is a  shimmering jewel of the cosmos an  earth-sized temperate paradise with a  healthy atmosphere and glistening seas  landing on the planet's surface it's a  balmy summer day yet despite the  seemingly ideal conditions there's not  as much terrestrial life on this planet  as one might expect look to the sky and  the reason becomes clear planet's lease  has no moon the lack of a moon might not  seem important to a planet's  habitability but moons can help  stabilize a planet's axial tilt keeping  the weather and temperature more  predictable which is essential to the  development of life the push and pull of  a moon's gravity also helps produce  intertidal zones regions that are key  for life to transition from water to  land for this reason it is probable that  most habitable planets in our universe  have at least one orbiting moon yet some  organisms have hauled themselves onto  the surface of solis all the same this  is santhir one of the planet's largest  land animals in a testament to the  stubbornness of evolution these odd  segmented creatures still live their  lives tied to the ocean as they have to  lay their eggs in the water like many  species of earth amphibians like frogs  they also have to keep their skin wet to  aid in respiration as sand ears don't  have fully functional terrestrial lungs  their burrows are reflective of their  dual lifestyle with both aquatic and  terrestrial entrances these nesting  grounds show that sand theirs are  surprisingly talented homemakers much  like beavers the semi-aquatic home  building champions of our own planet  these nests are vital when the weather  shifts planet salisa's unstable moonless  axial tilt means the climate is highly  variable with some regions frequently  buffeted by cyclones but not all  creatures fear the storm  this small lifeform is called a bihari  flyer and as its name suggests it's one  of the rare life forms on salis that has  managed to leave the water for the air  due to the intense weather these  creatures have rather extreme patterns  of migrations moving to the equator in  late spring to escape the raging summer  winds yet to the south it's a beautiful  clear night this planet is an incredible  testimony to life's persistence but it's  time we set off to our next destination  the next stop on our journey promises to  be an unforgettable one from space it is  clear that planet cralon is the site of  massive artificial engineering projects  it seems we have at last found advanced  alien life yet upon the surface  everything is quiet aside from sparse  colonies of bacteria  on the poles no biological life  whatsoever is present on this planet  instead these silent cities are ruled by  what we might term artificial life  a great hivemind of machines preside  over this planet which leaves one to  wonder where their makers have gone  perhaps the creators of this ai left the  planet for better prospects or perhaps  they died at the hands of their own  artificial creations and this desolate  world is the site of a mass grave  or is it these machines called the crail  might not fit our traditional standards  of life but they are at such a level of  advancement in interconnectivity they  could almost be considered an ecosystem  and an ecosystem can be studied at the  base of this highly unusual environment  are scouts small 2-3 foot drones scouts  appear to be in charge of finding  regions in the system to mine for  materials to make more crail these  mining operations are massive as the  machines have almost completely hollowed  out a neighboring moon the energy needed  to complete these operations is possible  thanks to another important life form so  to speak these crawl machines are solar  panels 600 foot long power collectors  which orbit the sun and transmit vital  energy to other machines this division  of labor means the crayol collective  function almost like a beehive  and the queens of this colony are the  archships by far the largest type of  machine archships are 20 miles or 32  kilometers in length  overall arc ships seem to function like  neurons in a brain constantly beaming  untranslatable information to other  machines and to each other indeed it's  possible that the entire crayola society  is better understood as a single solar  system spanning life form all with a  single goal  but what is this goal  are the creole mining for their own  purposes or are they merely following a  preset path  still active from countless years ago  fears of evil robots aside they don't  seem to be actively malicious yet at the  same time we might feel safer once we've  left this system but are cold machines  the only type of intelligence we'll find  on our journey our next stop is tavari  and from space we can see light shining  on the surface is this the glow of a  kindred civilization or something else  the only way to find out is to get  closer making our descent we find tavari  is much like other habitable planets we  visited save for one major difference  tivati isn't actually a planet at all  but a moon orbiting a gas giant similar  to jupiter and this moon is considerably  smaller than earth with a fraction of  the gravity and without the constraints  of a strong gravitational pull life on  this moon can become gigantic this is a  tevet a creature which at 30 feet or  nine meters tall wouldn't be able to  stand up on earth but they're perfect  for tavare where they spend their time  grazing on the tops of tall trees  meaning their lifestyle is similar to a  giraffe's yet tevets would tower over a  giraffe of earth which is why it's lucky  for us these titans are gentle giants  and with their vaguely humanoid body  plan is it possible that these are our  light makers it doesn't seem so for  while the tevans aren't unintelligent  they're a long way from civilization  building  here at last on the horizon we can  glimpse immense technological structures  these gleaming wonders are home to a  sapient biological species the first  ever encountered apart from ourselves  meet the yatuans a hexapod species that  don't look much like us at a glance but  have all the necessary ingredients for  high intelligence possessing both a  highly developed brain and manipulatory  appendages with two opposable thumb-like  structures yetuans are kindred spirits  in the story of evolution indeed they've  been at this for longer and are quite a  bit more advanced technologically than  we are and luckily for us are quite  friendly  translating their messages we learn  their buildings on tavaty are just small  colonies the yaetuan's planet of origin  is far more inspiring  with our new friends in tow we've  arrived at the yaetuan home world the  final destination of our interstellar  expedition this is yetu the gleaming  center of a network of yetun's  settlements that spans many star systems  landing in the middle of a bustling  metropolis we can see yaichuan citizens  going about their lives like a colony of  ants yetuans are more communal than  humans and seem to live in remarkable  harmony but it wasn't always this way  like us yaichuans have a long and  complicated past stretching from  hunter-gatherers to an industrial era  where the yetuans pushed their planet to  ecological collapse due to environmental  neglect  it is for this reason that yetuans fled  to build colonies on other planets in  mass exodus  today the billions of yatuans call  different worlds home and over thousands  of years have become subtly different  from each other both in language and  appearance  now however their society is  reconnecting as they've restored their  homeworld's environment using their  advanced technology  while the modern ecosystem of planet  yeh2 is of course dominated by the  yetuans there are other notable native  lifeforms worth discussing  the wetim are a native species of pack  hunters which have a close relationship  to the yatuans like humans and our own  canine companions the wetum are the  domestic dogs of planet yetu having been  selected for thousands of years to be  friendly and sociable  another culturally important animal are  the walteth four winged fliers that play  a vital role in pollinating plant life  on yehu including yetuan crops as a  result the etuwons encourage their  presence constructing artificial nests  on the sides of modern buildings to  ensure the cities don't disrupt their  habitat the yaetuan species is an  incredible story of redemption in many  ways the yaichuwan's journey from  planetary collapse to restoration is one  worth reflecting on will humanity repeat  their mistakes or learn from their  example only time will tell meeting and  communing with the yeti ones has been a  very special experience but now the time  has come for us to return home  we've seen much on our epic journey  through the stars  yet christian klein's book the teeming  universe contains many more speculative  worlds and alien creatures and i  encourage you to check out the link to  it below and part 1 of this series and  as always thanks for watching if you  enjoyed this entry please lend your  support and like subscribe and hit the  notification icon to stay up to date on  all things curious see you in the next  video  [Music]  you

